Every node can be described by a row and a column. I store those coordinates along with a visit number. Sorting by column first and row second puts the answer in the needed order. The visit number keeps nodes at the same position in their left-to-right order.

The sorting step makes the code a little longer, but it separates tree traversal from result building, which is useful for checking the ordering rules.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    private class Entry
    {
        public int Column;
        public int Row;
        public int Order;
        public int Value;

        public Entry(int column, int row, int order, int value)
        {
            Column = column;
            Row = row;
            Order = order;
            Value = value;
        }
    }

    public IList<IList<int>> VerticalTraversal(TreeNode root)
    {
        IList<IList<int>> result = new List<IList<int>>();
        if (root == null)
        {
            return result;
        }

        List<Entry> entries = new List<Entry>();
        int order = 0;
        Collect(root, 0, 0, ref order, entries);

        entries.Sort((first, second) =>
        {
            int byColumn = first.Column.CompareTo(second.Column);
            if (byColumn != 0) return byColumn;

            int byRow = first.Row.CompareTo(second.Row);
            if (byRow != 0) return byRow;

            return first.Order.CompareTo(second.Order);
        });

        int currentColumn = entries[0].Column;
        List<int> columnValues = new List<int>();

        foreach (Entry entry in entries)
        {
            if (entry.Column != currentColumn)
            {
                result.Add(columnValues);
                columnValues = new List<int>();
                currentColumn = entry.Column;
            }
            columnValues.Add(entry.Value);
        }

        result.Add(columnValues);
        return result;
    }

    private void Collect(TreeNode node, int row, int column,
                         ref int order, List<Entry> entries)
    {
        if (node == null)
        {
            return;
        }

        entries.Add(new Entry(column, row, order++, node.val));
        Collect(node.left, row + 1, column - 1, ref order, entries);
        Collect(node.right, row + 1, column + 1, ref order, entries);
    }
}
```

An empty root returns immediately, and a one-node tree produces one column. If there are N nodes, sorting takes O(N log N) time and the traversal takes O(N). The list of entries and recursion stack need O(N) space in the worst case. The input links are left unchanged.

This version uses recursion and stores two levels of information: the column and the row. A TreeMap keeps columns in sorted order, while another TreeMap keeps rows in top to bottom order. Values in the same row are appended from left to right during the traversal.

For an empty tree, the method returns an empty list. This also avoids trying to read a missing root.

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

class Solution {
    public List<List<Integer>> verticalTraversal(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        Map<Integer, Map<Integer, List<Integer>>> columns = new TreeMap<>();
        collect(root, 0, 0, columns);

        for (Map<Integer, List<Integer>> rows : columns.values()) {
            List<Integer> current = new ArrayList<>();
            for (List<Integer> values : rows.values()) {
                current.addAll(values);
            }
            answer.add(current);
        }
        return answer;
    }

    private void collect(TreeNode node, int row, int column,
                         Map<Integer, Map<Integer, List<Integer>>> columns) {
        if (node == null) {
            return;
        }

        columns.putIfAbsent(column, new TreeMap<>());
        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> rows = columns.get(column);
        rows.putIfAbsent(row, new ArrayList<>());
        rows.get(row).add(node.data);

        collect(node.left, row + 1, column - 1, columns);
        collect(node.right, row + 1, column + 1, columns);
    }
}
```

A small dry run with root 1 and children 2 and 3 creates groups -1: [2], 0: [1], and 1: [3], so the output is [[2], [1], [3]]. The time complexity is O(n log n) because of the ordered maps. The stored tree information takes O(n) space, not counting the recursion stack.

A queue keeps the tree in top to bottom order. Each node carries its column number, starting at 0 for the root. Moving left decreases the column and moving right increases it. The buckets are stored in a vector after finding the smallest and largest column.

For a tree with root 1, left child 2, and right child 3, the queue visits 1 at column 0, then 2 at -1 and 3 at 1. The final result is collected from the leftmost column to the rightmost one.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <queue>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    vector<vector<int> > verticalTraversal(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<vector<int> > result;
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return result;
        }

        queue<pair<TreeNode*, int> > pending;
        pending.push({root, 0});
        int smallest = 0;
        int largest = 0;

        while (!pending.empty()) {
            TreeNode* node = pending.front().first;
            int column = pending.front().second;
            pending.pop();

            smallest = min(smallest, column);
            largest = max(largest, column);

            if (node->left != nullptr) {
                pending.push({node->left, column - 1});
            }
            if (node->right != nullptr) {
                pending.push({node->right, column + 1});
            }
        }

        vector<vector<int> > columns(largest - smallest + 1);
        pending.push({root, 0});

        while (!pending.empty()) {
            TreeNode* node = pending.front().first;
            int column = pending.front().second;
            pending.pop();

            columns[column - smallest].push_back(node->data);

            if (node->left != nullptr) {
                pending.push({node->left, column - 1});
            }
            if (node->right != nullptr) {
                pending.push({node->right, column + 1});
            }
        }

        return columns;
    }
};
```

The tree is visited twice, so the time cost is O(n). The queue and column result use O(n) space. The input tree is not changed.

A level-order queue is handy here because nodes at a smaller depth are removed first. Each queue item carries its column, and a TreeMap stores the columns from left to right. Nodes that land in the same column are simply added in queue order.

The queue stores the children after their parent, so the output already follows the vertical order without a separate sort.

```java
import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.TreeMap;

class Solution {
    private static class State {
        TreeNode node;
        int column;

        State(TreeNode node, int column) {
            this.node = node;
            this.column = column;
        }
    }

    public List<List<Integer>> verticalTraversal(TreeNode root) {
        List<List<Integer>> answer = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) {
            return answer;
        }

        Map<Integer, List<Integer>> columns = new TreeMap<>();
        Deque<State> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.addLast(new State(root, 0));

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            State current = queue.removeFirst();
            columns.putIfAbsent(current.column, new ArrayList<>());
            columns.get(current.column).add(current.node.data);

            if (current.node.left != null) {
                queue.addLast(new State(current.node.left, current.column - 1));
            }
            if (current.node.right != null) {
                queue.addLast(new State(current.node.right, current.column + 1));
            }
        }

        answer.addAll(columns.values());
        return answer;
    }
}
```

Dry run: with root 1, children 2 and 3, and node 4 as the right child of 2, the queue visits columns 0, -1, 1, then 0. The TreeMap changes that into columns -1, 0, 1, giving [2], [1, 4], [3].

The empty-tree case is handled before the queue is created. Each node enters and leaves the queue once, so the time cost is O(n log w), where w is the number of different columns. The queue and map together use O(n) space.

## Intuition
The problem is not about the traversal order of the tree.
It’s about assigning each node a position and then ordering nodes based on those positions. So once every node has its coordinates, the tree structure no longer matters it becomes a sorting problem.
Since traversal order isn’t important, I use a simple pre-order DFS to collect coordinates, then sort and group the nodes.

